>That intolerance was entirely caused by LaTeX's (unnecessary!?)
>fragilities

Well, LaTeX is trying to do a bit more with B. L. User's tokens than
your average TeX macro package.  I usually test macros to make sure
they work in two settings:

 * \let\protect=\string \message{STUFF}
 * \let\protect=\noexpand \edef\temp{STUFF}

If it survives those two, it'll probably survive most LaTeX commands.
If you think these are unnecessary, consider:

 a) how to write a token stream (which may include the contents of
    counters, marks, etc.) to a toc file.

 b) how to upper-case a token stream.

If you can do either of these without introducing fragility (or
all-caps fonts :-), congratulations!

It's not too difficult to define robust macros, with appropriate uses
of \protect in the macro definition.

>Is that too much to ask of 2e?

It's not too much to ask of LaTeX3...

